            <ArticleTitle>Assessing work-asthma interaction with Amazon Mechanical Turk.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>381-5</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1097/JOM.0000000000000360</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVES" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To illustrate the utility of crowdsourcing for occupational health surveillance.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Amazon Mechanical Turk was used to recruit and obtain information from employed persons with asthma, who answered questions about work-asthma interactions.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Data collection from 60 subjects required only a few hours. Participants spent on average 7 minutes responding to seven questions (one optional) and used an average of 708 words. Work exacerbation, interference of asthma with work, and suggested workplace accommodation are frequent (83% reported at least one interaction).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">The full spectrum of work-asthma interactions should be considered. Modern crowdsourcing methods have considerable potential as occupational health surveillance tools because of their effectiveness; efficiency and financial viability are additional important advantages.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
